From 7ceef2af89b7820f8e7632ff05444b9c1fbe279d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Romain Dolbeau <romain@dolbeau.org>
Date: Sun, 18 Oct 2026 12:57:04 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 09/11] m68k pmap (MULTIPROCESSOR): per-pmap locks instead of
 the kernel lock

- pm_lock (adaptive mutex) for each user pmap; pmap_klock (recursive
  spin lock) for the kernel pmap, which interrupt handlers may change,
  and around the changes of the kernel mappings of user PT pages;
  pmap_pv_slock for the pv lists and attributes.
- The pv operations (pmap_page_protect, pmap_clear_*, pmap_is_*) hold
  the pv lock and only try to lock the pmap of each mapping, backing off
  and starting again if that fails.
- Nothing sleeps with these locks held: pmap_enter() allocates without
  waiting and retries after uvm_wait() with no lock held; the pv entry
  it may need is allocated before locking (a pool that grows needs
  pmap_klock); PT pages, segment tables and pv entries that become free
  are freed at the end of the operation.  User PT pages have no UVM
  object (its lock may be held by a caller of the pmap); their count is
  found from their kernel mapping.
- PTE bits are changed with compare-and-swap, as the MMUs of the other
  CPUs set U and M at any time.
- pv entries come from a pool cache.

Also: gftty(4) checks the console magic ("+++++") to enter ddb.
